From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 18:25:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(control): desktop notifications on a ghostty pane
 (KD-22, GD-19)

libghostty dispatched .desktop_notification for OSC 777;notify and for
iTerm2's bare OSC 9;<text>, and GhosttyControlCore had no case for it, so a
program asking for a toast on a ghostty pane got nothing.

Raising the same ShowNotification event ControlCore raises is the whole job:
the toast, the rate limit between toasts, the rule that a pane you are looking
at does not notify you about itself, and clicking a toast to summon its window
all live in TerminalPage and know nothing about which engine raised the event.
An empty title is not papered over here either - ghostty leaves it empty for
OSC 9, which carries only a body, and Tab.cpp fills it in with the tab's own.

The user's switch is the profile's `compatibility.allowOSC777`, which is off
by default. It is translated to ghostty's `desktop-notifications`, whose
default is the opposite, so leaving it alone would have given a ghostty pane
toasts that the cascadia pane beside it, under the same profile, refuses.
The gate lives in the translator rather than in the action handler because
Surface.zig tests that config before dispatching the action: with it off the
pane does not do the work, and there is no second gate to disagree with the
first. The inverted defaults are pinned by a unit test.

A trace at the boundary comes with it. A notification has four places it can
be dropped - the profile, ghostty's rate limit, WT's focus rule and the toast
API - and only the first is visible from outside, so the line that says the
action reached WT is what tells the four apart. Sizes, not the text: the
debugger channel is the wrong place for what a program chose to say to a user.

Measured with scripts/probe-notify.ps1 on a portable build, both ways round:
allowed -> `[ghostty] notification title=11 body=14 chars`, matching the
sequence that was sent; refused -> nothing, with the other ghostty traces
still arriving so the silence is evidence rather than a probe that saw
nothing. unitControl 68 -> 69.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 34 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp             | 19 +++++++++++
 .../GhosttySettingsTests.cpp                  | 20 +++++++++++
 3 files changed, 73 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index b79502edc..381aa0894 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -549,6 +549,40 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             return true;
         }
 
+        // OSC 777;notify, and iTerm2's bare OSC 9;<text>. Everything a
+        // notification needs after this point is TerminalPage's and knows
+        // nothing about engines - the toast itself, the rate limit, the "do
+        // not notify about the pane you are already looking at" rule, and
+        // clicking the toast to summon the window - so raising the same event
+        // ControlCore raises is the whole job.
+        //
+        // An empty title is not a defect to paper over here: ghostty leaves it
+        // empty for OSC 9, which carries only a body, and Tab.cpp fills it in
+        // with the tab's own title.
+        //
+        // The user's switch is the profile's `compatibility.allowOSC777`,
+        // which GhosttySettingsTranslator hands to ghostty as
+        // `desktop-notifications`. A pane whose profile says no therefore
+        // never reaches this case at all - Surface.zig tests the config before
+        // dispatching the action - so there is deliberately no second gate
+        // here to disagree with the first.
+        case GHOSTTY_ACTION_DESKTOP_NOTIFICATION:
+        {
+            const auto title = til::u8u16(std::string_view{ action.action.desktop_notification.title });
+            const auto body = til::u8u16(std::string_view{ action.action.desktop_notification.body });
+            // A notification that does not appear has four places it can have
+            // been dropped - the profile, ghostty's own rate limit, WT's "you
+            // are looking at that pane already" rule, and the toast API itself -
+            // and only this one is ours. Sizes rather than the text: the
+            // debugger channel is the wrong place for what a program chose to
+            // say to the user.
+            _trace(L"notification title=%u body=%u chars", static_cast<unsigned>(title.size()), static_cast<unsigned>(body.size()));
+            _raiseOnUiThread([this, title = winrt::hstring{ title }, body = winrt::hstring{ body }]() {
+                ShowNotification.raise(*this, winrt::make<implementation::ShowNotificationEventArgs>(title, body));
+            });
+            return true;
+        }
+
         case GHOSTTY_ACTION_PROGRESS_REPORT:
         {
             const auto state = static_cast<size_t>(action.action.progress_report.state);
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
index 6e6d709f3..ce2410edd 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttySettingsTranslator.cpp
@@ -129,6 +129,25 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // clipboard - measured, because that was the regression to fear.
         set("selection-trim-line", "false");
 
+        // --- Desktop notifications ----------------------------------------
+        // WT's switch is the profile's `compatibility.allowOSC777`, and it is
+        // **off** by default: a program gets to raise a toast only if the user
+        // said it may. ghostty's `desktop-notifications` is the same switch
+        // with the opposite default, so leaving it alone would have given a
+        // ghostty pane notifications that the cascadia pane beside it, under
+        // the same profile, refuses.
+        //
+        // The gate belongs here rather than in the action handler because
+        // ghostty already has one in the right place: Surface.zig tests this
+        // config before dispatching the action, so with it off the pane does
+        // not do the work and the embedder has nothing to decline.
+        //
+        // One difference comes with turning it on, and it is a documented diff
+        // rather than a hidden one: ghostty also honours iTerm2's bare
+        // `OSC 9;<text>` as a notification, which WT's parser does not
+        // implement at all.
+        set("desktop-notifications", settings.AllowOscNotifications() ? "true" : "false");
+
         // --- Cursor -------------------------------------------------------
         // Found by hand: a ghostty pane drew a block where the cascadia pane
         // beside it drew a bar, because the profile's cursorShape was never
diff --git a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
index a62078f1c..175b406c9 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttySettingsTests.cpp
@@ -42,6 +42,7 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         TEST_METHOD(WordDelimitersReachTheEngine);
         TEST_METHOD(TripleClickHighlightsTheWholeLine);
         TEST_METHOD(UrlDetectionFollowsTheProfile);
+        TEST_METHOD(DesktopNotificationsFollowTheProfile);
         TEST_METHOD(CursorShapeIsForwarded);
         TEST_METHOD(IntenseTextStyleIsForwarded);
         TEST_METHOD(BackslashesAreDoubledForTheWordCharsFieldOnly);
@@ -235,6 +236,25 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
                        L"a profile that turns URL detection off must turn ghostty's off too");
     }
 
+    // The two engines default this setting opposite ways round, which is the
+    // whole reason it has to be forwarded rather than assumed. WT's
+    // `compatibility.allowOSC777` is off unless the user turns it on;
+    // ghostty's `desktop-notifications` is on unless it is turned off. Left
+    // alone, a ghostty pane raised toasts that the cascadia pane beside it,
+    // under the same profile, would have refused.
+    void GhosttySettingsTests::DesktopNotificationsFollowTheProfile()
+    {
+        const auto off = Control::implementation::TranslateGhosttySettings(*_settings());
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(_has(off, "desktop-notifications", "false"),
+                       L"WT's default is off, so ghostty's default must be turned off too");
+
+        auto settings = _settings();
+        settings->AllowOscNotifications(true);
+        const auto on = Control::implementation::TranslateGhosttySettings(*settings);
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(_has(on, "desktop-notifications", "true"),
+                       L"a profile that allows OSC 777 must let the engine raise one");
+    }
+
     // Found by hand: a ghostty pane drew a block cursor beside a cascadia pane
     // drawing a bar, because the profile's cursorShape was never forwarded.
     //
